<h2>Why the Act built its own service machinery</h2>
<p>A DV application under Section 12 typically seeks relief that cannot wait — a residence order to stop a dispossession, a protection order against continuing violence, interim maintenance for a household suddenly without income. Ordinary civil service, with its cycle of unserved summons and fresh process fees, would defeat that purpose. The legislature&rsquo;s answer was Section 13: the Magistrate fixes the first date, and the notice travels through the Protection Officer, a statutory functionary answerable to the court, within a tightly drawn timeline.</p>

<h2>The Section 13 sequence</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Application filed, date fixed.</strong> On a Section 12 application, the Magistrate fixes the first hearing — the Act contemplates a first date ordinarily within three days of the court receiving the application.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Notice to the Protection Officer.</strong> The court issues notice of that date to the Protection Officer, who is responsible for getting it served on the respondent, and on any other person the Magistrate directs.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Two days to serve.</strong> The officer must get the notice served within a maximum of two days of receiving it, or within such further reasonable time as the Magistrate may allow — a deliberately short leash.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Declaration of service.</strong> The officer then files a declaration of service, which Section 13(2) treats as proof of service upon the respondent.</div>
</div>

<h2>The modes of service under Rule 12</h2>
<p>Rule 12 of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Rules, 2006 fleshes out the mechanics. The notice may be served by the Protection Officer himself or through persons acting at his direction — in practice this frequently means the beat police of the respondent&rsquo;s area. Service is attempted at the respondent&rsquo;s residence or place of employment, and where personal delivery proves impossible, the familiar fallback modes of the general law — delivery to an adult member of the household, affixation after diligent attempts, and postal or courier despatch where the court permits — are pressed into use. The object throughout is substance over form: did the notice, by a reliable route, reach the respondent&rsquo;s knowledge?</p>

<h2>What each side should watch</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For the aggrieved person.</strong> Supply complete, current addresses — residence and workplace — and telephone numbers in the application itself. Incomplete addresses are the single largest cause of adjournments at the service stage, and each unserved date postpones interim relief that the Act intends to be swift.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For the respondent.</strong> Appearing promptly is almost always wiser than evading. Evasion rarely prevents anything: the court can proceed under Section 23(2) on affidavit, and an ex parte interim order — once passed — must then be displaced by an application, a harder road than opposing it in the first place.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>On proof of service.</strong> The declaration under Section 13(2) is presumptive proof. A respondent who genuinely was not served should place specific material on record — proof of residence elsewhere, employment records, travel documents — rather than rest on a bald denial that seldom persuades.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>On defective service.</strong> Service at a long-abandoned address, or on a stranger, remains open to challenge. But the remedy is to appear, point out the defect, and seek recall of any ex parte order — not to ignore the proceeding while orders accumulate.</div>
</div>

<h2>Evasion and its consequences</h2>
<p>Respondents sometimes calculate that dodging the process server buys time. Under this Act the calculation usually fails. First, the Magistrate may allow substituted modes, and the two-day discipline of Section 13 keeps the matter listed at short intervals rather than drifting. Second, Section 23(2) permits ex parte interim orders on the strength of the aggrieved person&rsquo;s affidavit alone, where the application discloses domestic violence or its likelihood. Third, a record of evasion colours the court&rsquo;s view of the respondent&rsquo;s bona fides when interim maintenance and residence orders are framed. The respondent who finally appears after months of avoidance begins the case on the back foot.</p>

<h2>Common service defects raised in practice</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Notice served at an address the respondent had demonstrably vacated before the application was filed.</p>
  <p>Service effected on a family member with no authority or occasion to pass the notice on — for instance, an estranged relative in another household.</p>
  <p>Affixation resorted to without the diligent prior attempts that justify it.</p>
  <p>The declaration of service silent on the mode, date or place of service, inviting scrutiny of what actually happened.</p>
  <p>Notice for one date served after that date had already passed, leaving the respondent unaware of the adjourned hearing.</p>
</div>

<h2>The strategic takeaway</h2>
<p>Service under the DV Act is not a clerical formality; it is the hinge on which the first phase of the case turns. For the applicant&rsquo;s side, meticulous addresses and early coordination with the Protection Officer convert the statute&rsquo;s speed into real interim orders. For the respondent&rsquo;s side, prompt appearance preserves every defence — including genuine objections to service itself — while evasion converts a defensible case into one governed by ex parte orders obtained in absence.</p>
<div class="note">The timelines in Section 12 and Section 13 are directory in the sense that courts may allow further reasonable time, but they set the tempo the statute expects. Parties who treat the service stage seriously — on either side — typically find the substantive hearing reached months earlier than those who do not.</div>
